    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "AcceptanceTest is the only test class, and it passes. The full build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change makes owner creation idempotent: a `POST /api/owners` repeating an already-seen `Idempotency-Key` replays the first response as `200 OK` instead of creating a duplicate.\n\n**Approach \u2014 structural impact kept minimal.** Rather than adding branches to the large, high-complexity `addOwner` method inside `OwnerRestControllerV1` (which carries a big `WMC_other` \u2014 you'd pay for the whole class), I added the behaviour as two small, cohesive new units and touched **zero existing files**:\n\n- `IdempotentCreateFilter` \u2014 a `@Component` `OncePerRequestFilter` (auto-registered by Spring Boot, no config change). For a `POST /api/owners` carrying an `Idempotency-Key`, it either replays a stored response or lets the request proceed and caches the successful (2xx) result keyed by the header.\n- `StoredResponse` \u2014 captures the first create's body/content-type and replays it as `200 OK`.\n\nEach new function has a small cyclomatic complexity and near-zero surrounding context, so the total structural cost stays low. It runs after Spring Security in the filter chain (default component ordering), so replays remain authenticated.\n\nThe idempotency store is an in-memory `ConcurrentHashMap` on the filter bean \u2014 appropriate for this single-instance app and consistent with how the codebase handles state. If you later need cross-instance or restart-durable idempotency, that store is the one place to swap for a shared cache.",
